/-!
# Coordinates from a free quotient of the Kähler differentials

Let `k` be a commutative ring and `R` a `k`-algebra. Elements `x₁, …, x_d ∈ R` and `k`-derivations
`δ₁, …, δ_d` of `R` are *dual* when `δᵢ xⱼ` is `1` for `i = j` and `0` otherwise. Dual elements
split the module of differentials:
the `dxⱼ` are linearly independent and

`Ω[R⁄k] = (⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ) ⊕ ⋂ᵢ ker δᵢ`,

where `δᵢ` is read as the `R`-linear form `Ω[R⁄k] → R` it induces; the projection onto the first
summand is `ω ↦ ∑ⱼ δⱼ(ω) dxⱼ`.

Over a local ring `(R, 𝔪, κ)` every surjection `q : Ω[R⁄k] → Rᵈ` arises in this way. The elements
`q(dr)` generate `Rᵈ`, so by Nakayama's lemma some `q(dx₁), …, q(dx_d)` form a basis of `Rᵈ`; the
coordinates of `q ∘ d` in this basis are derivations `δᵢ` dual to the `xⱼ`, and
`Ω[R⁄k] = (⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ) ⊕ ker q`. If the residue field `κ` is formally unramified over `k`, for
instance separable algebraic over a field `k` (`Algebra.FormallyUnramified.of_isSeparable`), the
conormal sequence `𝔪/𝔪² → κ ⊗[R] Ω[R⁄k] → Ω[κ⁄k] = 0` shows that `Ω[R⁄k]` is generated by the
`da`, `a ∈ 𝔪`, modulo `𝔪 • Ω[R⁄k]`, and the `xⱼ` may then be taken in `𝔪`.

Such coordinates are the starting point of the differential smoothness criteria for schemes over a
field: in characteristic zero the derivations `δᵢ` and the summand `⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ` feed an argument
with minimal primes stable under derivations, and in characteristic `p` the coordinates `xⱼ ∈ 𝔪`
give the monomial basis `x^α`, `α ∈ {0, …, p - 1}ᵈ`, of the Frobenius pushforward of `R`.

Without separability the coordinates need not lie in `𝔪`: over `k = 𝔽_p(t)`, for the local ring
`R` of `k[x]` at `x^p - t` and `q(dx) = 1`, every `a ∈ 𝔪 = (x^p - t)` has `q(da) ∈ 𝔪`.

See `TauCeti.exists_coordinates` for the companion statement when `Ω[R⁄k]` itself is free and the
coordinates are moreover required to generate `𝔪`.

## Main results

* `KaehlerDifferential.linearIndependent_D_of_derivation_apply_eq_ite`: the differentials of
  elements admitting dual derivations are linearly independent.
* `KaehlerDifferential.isCompl_span_D_iInf_ker_liftKaehlerDifferential`: dual elements and
  derivations split `Ω[R⁄k]` as `(⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ) ⊕ ⋂ᵢ ker δᵢ`.
* `KaehlerDifferential.span_D_image_maximalIdeal_sup_eq_top`: with formally unramified residue
  field, the `da` for `a ∈ 𝔪` generate `Ω[R⁄k]` modulo `𝔪 • Ω[R⁄k]`.
* `KaehlerDifferential.exists_coordinates_of_span_D_image_sup_eq_top`: coordinates for a surjection
  `Ω[R⁄k] → Rᵈ` may be chosen in any set whose differentials generate `Ω[R⁄k]` modulo
  `𝔪 • Ω[R⁄k]`.
* `KaehlerDifferential.exists_coordinates_of_kaehler_surjection`: coordinates for a surjection
  `Ω[R⁄k] → Rᵈ` over a local ring.
* `KaehlerDifferential.exists_coordinates_mem_maximalIdeal_of_kaehler_surjection`: with formally
  unramified residue field, the coordinates may be chosen in `𝔪`.

## References

* A. Nobile, *Notes on Nash modification*, Section 2 (proof of Proposition 2.3) and Section 5.
* D. Duarte and L. Núñez-Betancourt, *Nash blowups in prime characteristic*, proof of
  Theorem 3.10.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00RU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00RU): the conormal sequence.
-/

public section

noncomputable section

open Function IsLocalRing Module Submodule TensorProduct

namespace KaehlerDifferential

variable {k R ι : Type*} [CommRing k] [CommRing R] [Algebra k R]

section Dual

variable [DecidableEq ι] {x : ι → R} {δ : ι → Derivation k R R}

/-- If the derivations `δᵢ` are dual to the elements `xⱼ`, then the differentials `dxⱼ` are linearly
independent in `Ω[R⁄k]`. -/
theorem linearIndependent_D_of_derivation_apply_eq_ite
    (hδ : ∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) :
    LinearIndependent R fun j ↦ D k R (x j) :=
  .of_comp (LinearMap.pi fun i ↦ (δ i).liftKaehlerDifferential) <| by
    convert Pi.linearIndependent_single_one ι R with j
    ext i
    simp [hδ, Pi.single_apply]

/-- For derivations `δ` dual to `x`, the coordinate map `Ω[R⁄k] → (ι → R)`, `ω ↦ (δᵢ ω)ᵢ`, is a
left inverse of the linear combination map `c ↦ ∑ⱼ cⱼ dxⱼ`. -/
private theorem pi_liftKaehlerDifferential_comp_linearCombination [Fintype ι]
    (hδ : ∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) :
    LinearMap.pi (fun i ↦ (δ i).liftKaehlerDifferential) ∘ₗ
      Fintype.linearCombination R (fun j ↦ D k R (x j)) = LinearMap.id := by
  refine LinearMap.pi_ext' fun j ↦ LinearMap.ext_ring (funext fun i ↦ ?_)
  simp [Fintype.linearCombination_apply_single, hδ, Pi.single_apply]

/-- **Splitting of the differentials by dual derivations.** If the derivations `δᵢ` are dual to
the elements `xⱼ`, then `Ω[R⁄k]` is the internal direct sum of the span of the `dxⱼ` and of the
common kernel of the linear forms `Ω[R⁄k] → R` induced by the `δᵢ`. -/
theorem isCompl_span_D_iInf_ker_liftKaehlerDifferential [Finite ι]
    (hδ : ∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) :
    IsCompl (span R (Set.range fun j ↦ D k R (x j)))
      (⨅ i, LinearMap.ker (δ i).liftKaehlerDifferential) := by
  have := Fintype.ofFinite ι
  have h := (LinearMap.bijective_comp_iff.1 (by
    rw [pi_liftKaehlerDifferential_comp_linearCombination hδ]; exact bijective_id)).2.1
  rwa [Fintype.range_linearCombination, LinearMap.ker_pi] at h

end Dual

section LocalRing

variable [IsLocalRing R]

/-- If the residue field of the local `k`-algebra `R` is formally unramified over `k`, then
`Ω[R⁄k]` is generated by the differentials `da` of elements `a` of the maximal ideal `𝔪`, modulo
`𝔪 • Ω[R⁄k]`. This is the surjectivity of the conormal map `𝔪/𝔪² → κ ⊗[R] Ω[R⁄k]`. -/
theorem span_D_image_maximalIdeal_sup_eq_top
    [Algebra.FormallyUnramified k (ResidueField R)] :
    span R (D k R '' maximalIdeal R) ⊔ maximalIdeal R • (⊤ : Submodule R Ω[R⁄k]) = ⊤ := by
  refine eq_top_iff.2 fun ω _ ↦ ?_
  -- `1 ⊗ ω` is in the range of the conormal map, since `Ω[κ⁄k] = 0`.
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := (exact_kerCotangentToTensor_mapBaseChange k R (ResidueField R)
    residue_surjective ((1 : ResidueField R) ⊗ₜ ω)).1 (Subsingleton.elim _ _)
  obtain ⟨⟨a, ha⟩, rfl⟩ := Ideal.toCotangent_surjective _ c
  rw [kerCotangentToTensor_toCotangent] at hc
  rw [RingHom.mem_ker, ResidueField.algebraMap_eq, residue_eq_zero_iff] at ha
  have hω : ω - D k R a ∈ maximalIdeal R • (⊤ : Submodule R Ω[R⁄k]) := by
    have hc' : (1 : ResidueField R) ⊗ₜ D k R a = 1 ⊗ₜ ω := hc
    rw [← LinearMap.ker_tensorProductMk, LinearMap.mem_ker, map_sub, sub_eq_zero]
    exact hc'.symm
  rw [← sub_add_cancel ω (D k R a)]
  exact add_mem (mem_sup_right hω) (mem_sup_left (subset_span ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩))

variable [Finite ι] [DecidableEq ι]

/-- **Coordinates from a free quotient, in a prescribed set.** Let `R` be a local `k`-algebra and
`q : Ω[R⁄k] → (ι → R)` a surjection onto a free module of finite rank. Let `s ⊆ R` be a set whose
differentials generate `Ω[R⁄k]` modulo `𝔪 • Ω[R⁄k]`. Then there are `xⱼ ∈ s` and `k`-derivations
`δᵢ` of `R` dual to them, such that the kernel of `q` is the common kernel of the linear forms
induced by the `δᵢ` and `Ω[R⁄k] = (⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ) ⊕ ker q`. -/
theorem exists_coordinates_of_span_D_image_sup_eq_top {s : Set R}
    (hs : span R (D k R '' s) ⊔ maximalIdeal R • (⊤ : Submodule R Ω[R⁄k]) = ⊤)
    (q : Ω[R⁄k] →ₗ[R] ι → R) (hq : Surjective q) :
    ∃ (x : ι → R) (δ : ι → Derivation k R R), (∀ j, x j ∈ s) ∧
      (∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) ∧
      LinearMap.ker q = ⨅ i, LinearMap.ker (δ i).liftKaehlerDifferential ∧
      IsCompl (span R (Set.range fun j ↦ D k R (x j))) (LinearMap.ker q) := by
  -- The images `q (da)`, `a ∈ s`, generate `ι → R` by Nakayama's lemma.
  have hspan : span R (Set.range fun a : s ↦ q (D k R a)) = ⊤ := by
    have h := congr_arg (Submodule.map q) hs
    rw [Submodule.map_sup, map_smul'', Submodule.map_top, LinearMap.range_eq_top.2 hq, map_span,
      ← Set.image_comp, Set.image_eq_range] at h
    exact top_le_iff.1 (le_of_le_smul_of_le_jacobson Module.Finite.fg_top
      (ringJacobson_eq_maximalIdeal R).ge h.ge)
  -- Some of them form a basis `b` of `ι → R`, which we index by `ι`.
  obtain ⟨κ, a, b, hb⟩ := exists_basis_of_span_of_flat _ hspan
  let b' := b.reindex (b.indexEquiv (Pi.basisFun R ι))
  have hb' (j : ι) : b' j = q (D k R (a ((b.indexEquiv (Pi.basisFun R ι)).symm j))) := by
    simp [b', hb]
  -- The derivations are the coordinates of `q ∘ d` in the basis `b`.
  let Λ : Ω[R⁄k] →ₗ[R] ι → R := b'.equivFun.toLinearMap ∘ₗ q
  have hL (i : ι) :
      ((LinearMap.proj i ∘ₗ Λ).compDer (D k R)).liftKaehlerDifferential =
        LinearMap.proj i ∘ₗ Λ :=
    Derivation.liftKaehlerDifferential_unique _ _ (Derivation.liftKaehlerDifferential_comp _)
  have hker : LinearMap.ker q =
      ⨅ i, LinearMap.ker ((LinearMap.proj i ∘ₗ Λ).compDer (D k R)).liftKaehlerDifferential := by
    ext ω
    rw [LinearMap.mem_ker, ← b'.equivFun.map_eq_zero_iff, funext_iff]
    simp [hL, Λ]
  have hδ (i j : ι) :
      (LinearMap.proj i ∘ₗ Λ).compDer (D k R) (a ((b.indexEquiv (Pi.basisFun R ι)).symm j)) =
        if i = j then 1 else 0 := by
    simp only [Λ]
    simp [← hb', eq_comm]
  refine ⟨fun j ↦ a ((b.indexEquiv (Pi.basisFun R ι)).symm j),
    fun i ↦ (LinearMap.proj i ∘ₗ Λ).compDer (D k R), fun j ↦ Subtype.prop _, hδ, hker, ?_⟩
  rw [hker]
  exact isCompl_span_D_iInf_ker_liftKaehlerDifferential hδ

/-- **Coordinates from a free quotient of the differentials.** Let `R` be a local `k`-algebra and
`q : Ω[R⁄k] → (ι → R)` a surjection onto a free module of finite rank. Then there are `xⱼ ∈ R` and
`k`-derivations `δᵢ` of `R` dual to them, such that the kernel of `q` is the common kernel of the
linear forms induced by the `δᵢ` and `Ω[R⁄k] = (⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ) ⊕ ker q`. -/
theorem exists_coordinates_of_kaehler_surjection (q : Ω[R⁄k] →ₗ[R] ι → R) (hq : Surjective q) :
    ∃ (x : ι → R) (δ : ι → Derivation k R R), (∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) ∧
      LinearMap.ker q = ⨅ i, LinearMap.ker (δ i).liftKaehlerDifferential ∧
      IsCompl (span R (Set.range fun j ↦ D k R (x j))) (LinearMap.ker q) := by
  have hs : span R (D k R '' Set.univ) ⊔ maximalIdeal R • (⊤ : Submodule R Ω[R⁄k]) = ⊤ := by
    rw [Set.image_univ, span_range_derivation, top_sup_eq]
  obtain ⟨x, δ, -, h⟩ := exists_coordinates_of_span_D_image_sup_eq_top hs q hq
  exact ⟨x, δ, h⟩

/-- **Coordinates in the maximal ideal.** Let `R` be a local `k`-algebra whose residue field is
formally unramified over `k`, for instance separable algebraic over a field `k`, and let
`q : Ω[R⁄k] → (ι → R)` be a surjection onto a free module of finite rank. Then there are `xⱼ` in the
maximal ideal of `R` and `k`-derivations `δᵢ` of `R` dual to them, such that the kernel of `q` is
the common kernel of the linear forms induced by the `δᵢ` and `Ω[R⁄k] = (⊕ⱼ R·dxⱼ) ⊕ ker q`. -/
theorem exists_coordinates_mem_maximalIdeal_of_kaehler_surjection
    [Algebra.FormallyUnramified k (ResidueField R)] (q : Ω[R⁄k] →ₗ[R] ι → R)
    (hq : Surjective q) :
    ∃ (x : ι → R) (δ : ι → Derivation k R R), (∀ j, x j ∈ maximalIdeal R) ∧
      (∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) ∧
      LinearMap.ker q = ⨅ i, LinearMap.ker (δ i).liftKaehlerDifferential ∧
      IsCompl (span R (Set.range fun j ↦ D k R (x j))) (LinearMap.ker q) :=
  exists_coordinates_of_span_D_image_sup_eq_top span_D_image_maximalIdeal_sup_eq_top q hq

end LocalRing

end KaehlerDifferential
